. * Notes: one text, wrapped into lines of at most 138 characters (PDF text runs wider than the PNG)
. local ntext "Notes: Firm links are fictional in the development data, so who stays, leaves or comes back means nothing
>  here; read this chart on the real BPS files. Every firm in the `cohort_year' file, looked up in the next three files 
> (`f1', `f2', `f3'); percent = share of the `cohort_year' firms. Stayed = in all three; workers in `f3' against `cohort
> _year', about the same = within `band_pct'% either way (output, `outword', in the companion table). Absent, then back 
> = missed `f1' or `f2' but in `f3' again (`n_back' firms here); the `f3' row = Stayed plus these firms. Left is not pro
> of of closure: the files record no closures, so a firm may have closed, fallen out of the survey, stopped answering, o
> r may return after `f3'. Counts only, not the effect of COVID (Figure R4-02 shows normal years)."

. local notelines ""

. local line ""

. foreach w of local ntext {
  2.     if ustrlen("`line' `w'") > 138 & "`line'" != "" {
  3.         local notelines `"`notelines' "`line'""'
  4.         local line "`w'"
  5.     }
  6.     else local line = strtrim("`line' `w'")
  7. }

. local notelines `"`notelines' "`line'""'

. display `"   note lines: `notelines'"'
   note lines:  "Notes: Firm links are fictional in the development data, so who stays, leaves or comes back means nothi
> ng here; read this chart on the" "real BPS files. Every firm in the 2019 file, looked up in the next three files (2020
> , 2021, 2022); percent = share of the 2019 firms." "Stayed = in all three; workers in 2022 against 2019, about the sam
> e = within 10% either way (output, current prices, in the companion" "table). Absent, then back = missed 2020 or 2021 
> but in 2022 again (0 firms here); the 2022 row = Stayed plus these firms. Left is not" "proof of closure: the files re
> cord no closures, so a firm may have closed, fallen out of the survey, stopped answering, or may return" "after 2022. 
> Counts only, not the effect of COVID (Figure R4-02 shows normal years)."

